WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.379
Okay, so we've talked a lot about specific lighting setups,

2
00:00:04.379 --> 00:00:06.099
but we've not really talked too much about reflections.

3
00:00:06.099 --> 00:00:08.859
So part of what makes a convincing lighting solution

4
00:00:08.859 --> 00:00:10.500
are gonna be the reflections.

5
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:11.699
So let's go ahead and take a look

6
00:00:11.699 --> 00:00:12.900
at what we're gonna be looking at

7
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.320
with this section of the course.

8
00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.559
I will be taking a look at reflection pros themselves

9
00:00:17.559 --> 00:00:18.960
and when to use them,

10
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.079
the types of reflections that you can get,

11
00:00:21.079 --> 00:00:22.879
scenarios for reflections,

12
00:00:22.879 --> 00:00:25.000
the building quality of the reflections themselves

13
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.160
and some tips and tricks.

14
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.579
So when should we use reflection captures?

15
00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.770
They're to be used with primarily reflective surfaces

16
00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:33.890
more than anything.

17
00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:35.689
And the idea is to give the illusion

18
00:00:35.689 --> 00:00:38.649
of a believable reflective material or substance.

19
00:00:38.649 --> 00:00:41.070
If you do not use the reflection capture actor,

20
00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:42.329
as you can see here,

21
00:00:42.329 --> 00:00:44.109
Unreal is gonna go ahead

22
00:00:44.109 --> 00:00:46.570
and use screen space reflection as a default.

23
00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.530
Using a reflection capture will create

24
00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:52.289
and give you more detailed believable reflections.

25
00:00:52.289 --> 00:00:53.969
And you can find this actor

26
00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:56.810
under modes and visual effects.

27
00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.250
The types that can be used are the followed,

28
00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:01.250
screen space reflections that are gonna be the default,

29
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:02.810
spherical reflections,

30
00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:05.209
box reflections and planar reflections.

31
00:01:05.209 --> 00:01:06.290
Now it's important to understand

32
00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:08.489
that reflection captures are only needed

33
00:01:08.489 --> 00:01:10.890
when you're using light mass and bacon lighting.

34
00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.689
They are not needed for ray traced reflections,

35
00:01:13.689 --> 00:01:15.549
but they can help.

36
00:01:15.549 --> 00:01:18.989
They have real time reflections when using ray tracing

37
00:01:18.989 --> 00:01:20.430
and no re-bacon is needed,

38
00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:21.989
but a hybrid of both can be used

39
00:01:21.989 --> 00:01:24.430
to help performance if you need to.

40
00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.109
Now, what type of reflections can you have?

41
00:01:26.109 --> 00:01:27.849
You have screen space reflections.

42
00:01:27.849 --> 00:01:31.829
So it's Unreal Engine 5's old default reflections system.

43
00:01:31.829 --> 00:01:33.030
We now use Lumen.

44
00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:37.540
Lumen reflections are actually now gonna be on

45
00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.280
probably by default,

46
00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.680
but you can edit them in this case

47
00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.400
by creating a post-process volume

48
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.219
and you can control the intensity, quality and roughness.

49
00:01:49.879 --> 00:01:51.280
To affect the whole scene,

50
00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:52.939
you just need to set your post-process volume

51
00:01:52.939 --> 00:01:54.700
to infinite bounce.

52
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.319
And it's just one column below

53
00:01:56.319 --> 00:01:57.819
on the screen space settings.

54
00:02:00.129 --> 00:02:02.049
You've got sphere and box reflections.

55
00:02:02.049 --> 00:02:04.049
They're used when baking lights.

56
00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:05.810
You can control the influence and radius

57
00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:09.650
of how strong the range of reflection can be.

58
00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:11.969
By default, the source type is set to captured scene

59
00:02:11.969 --> 00:02:14.710
and you can customize the reflection via a cube map

60
00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:16.129
if you wanna add your own cube map

61
00:02:16.129 --> 00:02:17.729
that you've generated yourself.

62
00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:20.789
It's important to note that the reflection capture

63
00:02:20.789 --> 00:02:22.430
will use the texture as a source

64
00:02:23.789 --> 00:02:25.590
and the brightness can be controlled

65
00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:30.479
if you find that your reflections are a little bit too dark.

66
00:02:30.479 --> 00:02:33.000
So it is a fairly versatile system.

67
00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:35.939
We also got planar reflections.

68
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:40.520
UE4 and now UE5 also have support

69
00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.099
for real-time planar reflections,

70
00:02:42.099 --> 00:02:45.120
which can give you more accurate reflections

71
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:46.800
than screen space reflections.

72
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:47.840
And they're actually really good

73
00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:50.199
for flat surfaces like water, for example.

74
00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:54.000
The higher render cost

75
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.360
this particular type of reflection is due to how they work.

76
00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.240
They actually render the level again

77
00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.000
on the opposite direction of the reflection.

78
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.560
So that's one thing to keep in mind

79
00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.199
that planar reflection is fairly expensive

80
00:03:07.199 --> 00:03:09.159
due to its nature.

81
00:03:09.159 --> 00:03:10.759
You also got scenarios.

82
00:03:12.919 --> 00:03:14.580
The use case for these is pretty logical

83
00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:15.639
based on the reflection type.

84
00:03:15.639 --> 00:03:18.750
If your model is square in nature

85
00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:20.530
and the reflection is close to the original shape,

86
00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:22.490
you can use a box reflection

87
00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:26.740
and then you can also use a sphere reflection

88
00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:29.719
if you have some more curved surfaces

89
00:03:29.719 --> 00:03:31.879
or highly distorted surfaces

90
00:03:31.879 --> 00:03:35.759
like puddles, water, glasses, glass sculpture,

91
00:03:35.759 --> 00:03:37.919
anything like that that you can think of.

92
00:03:37.919 --> 00:03:40.919
The sphere capture will always have a curve on the edge

93
00:03:40.919 --> 00:03:42.759
that may be seen at the right angle.

94
00:03:42.759 --> 00:03:44.319
But again, keep that in mind.

95
00:03:44.319 --> 00:03:47.000
It's if you don't have a highly reflective surface,

96
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:47.960
it's not gonna be a huge issue,

97
00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.740
but just keep that in mind.

98
00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:52.139
Now, when it comes to quality control

99
00:03:52.139 --> 00:03:55.099
and building settings that we need to keep in mind,

100
00:03:56.389 --> 00:03:57.669
every time you move, create or delete

101
00:03:57.669 --> 00:04:00.509
a reflection capture, you'll have to rebuild

102
00:04:00.509 --> 00:04:04.430
because they get all converted into an atlas essentially.

103
00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:07.509
Unreal Engine will automatically rebuild reflections

104
00:04:07.509 --> 00:04:08.349
when you build lights.

105
00:04:08.349 --> 00:04:10.919
So that's something to keep in mind.

106
00:04:10.919 --> 00:04:12.520
So, and this is kind of handy as well,

107
00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:14.759
but you can build reflections on their own.

108
00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:17.420
To control the resolution of your reflections,

109
00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:18.939
you need to go to your project settings

110
00:04:19.139 --> 00:04:22.620
and set them to 1K or 2K if you need higher quality.

111
00:04:23.899 --> 00:04:27.300
But this is something you really only need

112
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:31.480
if you're trying to go for a photorealistic setting

113
00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:37.199
and also if you're using a ray trace hybrid.

114
00:04:37.199 --> 00:04:38.920
So, tips that I can give you

115
00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:40.420
when it comes to reflections.

116
00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:44.839
They're not calculated in any ray trace scenario.

117
00:04:44.839 --> 00:04:47.680
So it's a bit of an illusion of detail.

118
00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:50.620
But again, it's a good fallback to get quality.

119
00:04:51.459 --> 00:04:54.899
But overall, it's useful for baking lights.

120
00:04:54.899 --> 00:04:56.540
When custom making your own reflections,

121
00:04:56.540 --> 00:05:00.100
just keep in mind, do not go too high with the resolution.

122
00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.459
Keeping it at 1K or 2K is the most ideal

123
00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:06.100
since the higher resolution, the higher the cost.

124
00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:08.660
And in most cases, unless you have

125
00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:10.540
in mirror-like surfaces, you can get away

126
00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:12.500
with sort of blurrier reflections in general.

127
00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:13.899
So just keep that in mind.
